<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trần Trọng Kim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tran Trong Kim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trần Trọng Kim (1883–1953) was a Vietnamese scholar and historian, author of a widely read outline history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, who served as prime minister of the Japanese-backed Empire of Vietnam from April to August 1945. His government ended when Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> abdicated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a> led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tran-trong-kim.jpg" alt="Trần Trọng Kim" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mr._Tran_Trong_Kim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kim was born in 1883 in Nghi Xuân, Hà Tĩnh Province, in the French protectorate of Annam, a region that soon afterwards saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> movement of Phan Đình Phùng. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> at schools for the elite and began as an interpreter for the French administration in Ninh Bình. In 1905 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as an employee of a private company, and in 1908 a scholarship of the École Coloniale took him to the École Normale of Melun. From September 1911 he taught in Annam, and by 1942 he was an inspector of elementary public instruction in Tonkin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Kim was known for textbooks written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-alphabet">Vietnamese alphabet</a>, chữ Quốc Ngữ, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Vietnamese history. His two best-known works were Việt Nam sử lược (A Brief History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, 1920), which stressed Chinese influence on Vietnamese society, and Nho giáo (Confucianism, 1929-1933). The second strongly praised Confucianism, provoked wide debate on its place in Vietnamese life and was seen as a bridge between scholars raised under the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> and those of the French schools. He led Buddhist and Confucian associations and in 1939 joined the Chamber of People's Representatives in Tonkin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Contacts with Japanese scholars made him suspect to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-decoux">Admiral Jean Decoux</a>, and on 28 October 1943 Japanese agents took him under the protection of the Kenpeitai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. He was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, then recalled to Saigon on 30 March 1945, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had deposed the French.</p><p>On 7 April he at first refused the post of prime minister, saying he was too old and had no party, but on 16 April he agreed and the next day proposed a cabinet of ten ministers. The government was hampered by its short time, Japanese wartime control and Allied bombing. After it fell he returned to scholarship. Milton Sacks and John T. McAlister regarded him as a Japanese puppet; Trương Bửu Lâm saw his cabinet as apolitical technocrats.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trần Trọng Kim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bao Dai appointed a cabinet of non-communist intellectuals under the historian <b>Tran Trong Kim</b>, which reintroduced Vietnamese as the language of administration, revised school curricula and negotiated the return of Cochinchina and the cities of Hanoi, Haiphong and Da Nang from Japanese control.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-vietnam">Empire of Vietnam</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nghi Xuân, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-lat">Đà Lạt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.63,105.77&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Nghi Xuân on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Trần Trọng Kim"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3858 5895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3858 5895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.63,105.77&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
